{"task_id": "Mbpp/56", "solution": "def check(number: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a number is one less than twice its reverse.\n    The reverse operation preserves sign and drops leading zeros in the absolute value.\n    \"\"\"\n    def _reverse_number(num: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Reverses the decimal digits of an integer, preserving sign and dropping leading zeros.\"\"\"\n        if num == 0:\n            return 0\n\n        sign = -1 if num < 0 else 1\n        abs_num_str = str(abs(num))\n        reversed_abs_num_str = abs_num_str[::-1]\n        reversed_abs_num = int(reversed_abs_num_str)\n        return sign * reversed_abs_num\n\n    reversed_val = _reverse_number(number)\n    return number == (2 * reversed_val - 1)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/20", "solution": "def is_woodall(n: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if the given number is a Woodall number.\n    A Woodall number is defined as W_k = k * 2^k - 1 for integer k >= 0.\n    \"\"\"\n    MAX_ITER_K = 1024  # Limit iterations to prevent excessively long computations for very large n\n\n    if n < 0:\n        return False  # Woodall numbers are generally considered non-negative or -1 for k=0.\n                      # Per design, negative inputs are \"invalid\" and should not raise exceptions here.\n\n    target = n + 1  # We are checking for k * 2^k = n + 1\n    \n    for k in range(0, MAX_ITER_K + 1):\n        # Calculate k * 2^k\n        # (1 << k) is an efficient way to compute 2^k\n        candidate = k * (1 << k)\n\n        if candidate == target:\n            return True\n        if candidate > target:\n            # Since k * 2^k grows strictly for k >= 0,\n            # if the current candidate exceeds the target, no larger k will match.\n            return False\n            \n    return False # If no k is found within the iteration limit"}
