{"task_id": "Mbpp/428", "solution": "from typing import Sequence, List, TypeVar\nT = TypeVar(\"T\")\ndef shell_sort(array: Sequence[T]) -> List[T]:\n    \"\"\"Sorts an array using the Shell Sort algorithm.\n    This implementation uses Ciura's gap sequence.\n    \"\"\"\n    arr = list(array)  # Create a mutable copy of the input array for in-place sorting\n    n = len(arr)\n\n    # Generate Ciura's gap sequence\n    ciura_gaps = [701, 301, 132, 57, 23, 10, 4, 1]\n    gaps = [g for g in ciura_gaps if g < n]\n    if not gaps:\n        gaps = [1] # Ensure a gap of 1 if the array is too small for Ciura's larger gaps\n\n    for gap in gaps:\n        # Perform gapped insertion sort for the current gap size\n        for i in range(gap, n):\n            temp = arr[i]\n            j = i\n            # Shift elements that are greater than temp to the right\n            while j >= gap and arr[j - gap] > temp:\n                arr[j] = arr[j - gap]\n                j -= gap\n            arr[j] = temp\n    return arr"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/429", "solution": "def and_tuples(left_tuple: tuple[int, ...], right_tuple: tuple[int, ...]) -> tuple[int, ...]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the element-wise bitwise AND of two tuples.\n    Assumes inputs are tuples of integers of the same length.\n    \"\"\"\n    return tuple(a & b for a, b in zip(left_tuple, right_tuple))"}
