{"task_id": "Mbpp/471", "solution": "def find_remainder(arr, n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the product of elements in arr modulo n using modular multiplication.\n    Handles an empty array by treating its product as 1.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n <= 0:\n        # Based on design context, an InvalidModulusError would be raised here.\n        # However, per instructions, no exception raising and no isinstance checks.\n        # This branch effectively makes the output 0 if n is non-positive.\n        return 0\n\n    product = 1 % n\n    for x in arr:\n        product = (product * (x % n)) % n\n    return product"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/468", "solution": "def max_product(arr: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the maximum product of a strictly increasing subsequence within the array.\n    Uses dynamic programming to track maximum and minimum products ending at each index.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(arr)\n\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # max_prod[i] stores the maximum product of an increasing subsequence ending at index i\n    # min_prod[i] stores the minimum product of an increasing subsequence ending at index i\n    # This is necessary to handle negative numbers, as a small negative * another negative can become a large positive.\n    max_prod = list(arr)\n    min_prod = list(arr)\n\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i):\n            if arr[j] < arr[i]:  # Check for strictly increasing subsequence condition\n                # Candidate products formed by extending subsequences ending at j with arr[i]\n                cand1 = max_prod[j] * arr[i]\n                cand2 = min_prod[j] * arr[i]\n\n                # Update max_prod[i] with the maximum among its current value and the candidates\n                max_prod[i] = max(max_prod[i], cand1, cand2)\n                # Update min_prod[i] with the minimum among its current value and the candidates\n                min_prod[i] = min(min_prod[i], cand1, cand2)\n    \n    # The overall maximum product is the maximum value found in the max_prod array.\n    return max(max_prod)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/472", "solution": "def check_Consecutive(numbers):\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if the given list of numbers contains consecutive integers,\n    considering deduplication and sorting.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(numbers) <= 1:\n        return True\n\n    # Duplicates disqualify the list\n    if len(set(numbers)) != len(numbers):\n        return False\n\n    sorted_nums = sorted(numbers)\n\n    for i in range(len(sorted_nums) - 1):\n        if sorted_nums[i+1] - sorted_nums[i] != 1:\n            return False\n\n    return True"}
