{"task_id": "Mbpp/572", "solution": "import collections\ndef two_unique_nums(*lists: list) -> list:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns numbers that appear exactly once across all provided lists,\n    preserving their first-appearance order.\n    \"\"\"\n    \n    # Flatten all input lists into a single sequence while preserving order.\n    flattened = []\n    for sublist in lists:\n        flattened.extend(sublist)\n\n    # Count the frequency of each number in the flattened list.\n    freq = collections.Counter(flattened)\n\n    # Collect numbers that appear exactly once, maintaining their original order.\n    result = []\n    seen = set() # To prevent adding a number multiple times if it appears unique in multiple places (not an issue here if freq is 1)\n    for num in flattened:\n        if freq[num] == 1 and num not in seen:\n            result.append(num)\n            seen.add(num)\n            \n    return result"}
